@@ -123,6 +126,17 @@
 - **Same-cycle priority.** When two events can happen in the same cycle (for
   example a software write and an internal end-of-operation), decide from the
   RTL which one wins and make your assertion account for both.
+- **Conditions you rely on go in the antecedent.** The consequent (right of
+  `|->` / `|=>`) is a claim about the design, so it may only require things
+  the design controls. If a property holds only when the environment behaves
+  in a certain way (for example "no new write arrives while the operation
+  runs"), put that condition on the left side, e.g.
+  `start ##1 (!write)[*N] |-> done`, never inside the consequent: a legal
+  input that breaks it would then count as a violation.
+- **Bounded time.** Express "after N events / within N cycles" with bounded
+  forms (`##N`, `##[0:N]`, `[*N]`, a counter in auxiliary logic). Avoid
+  open-ended forms (`[->N]` reaching into an unbounded future,
+  `s_eventually`, `##[1:$]`) unless the documentation really gives no bound.
 - **Timing.** `a |-> b` checks `b` in the same cycle as `a`; `a |=> b` checks
   it in the next cycle; a register assigned with `<=` shows its new value one
   cycle after the condition. Check each alignment against the RTL by hand.
